# Data retention and anonymized analytics policy

Status: policy work intentionally deferred. Do not change retention behavior as
part of the account/workspace lifecycle fixes.

## Current destructive account-deletion behavior

- A self-service deletion request locks the account immediately and schedules
  purge after 30 days. Support cancellation inside that window clears the
  lifecycle lock; workspace membership, sharing, listings, editing, and cloning
  become available again because their structural records were kept dormant.
- Final purge deletes every workspace owned by the account, including shared
  workspaces, and therefore deletes their files, materials, revision history,
  collaboration state, share links, and workspace-scoped ingest/RAG records.
- Final purge deletes standalone materials owned by the account and the
  account's personal events, tasks, canvases, labels, conversations, tags,
  attempts, mistakes, notifications, notification preferences/outbox rows,
  lifecycle notices, source-import jobs, and stored model credentials/settings.
- Memberships and invitations involving the deleted account are removed.
  Authorship references in content owned by another account are set to null;
  editor mentions must fall back to their stored label or user id.
- The user row remains as a scrubbed tombstone so foreign-key attribution and
  external billing correlation remain stable. Name, email, avatar, class label,
  and streak are cleared. The Stripe customer id is currently retained.
- Webhook event bodies associated with the user are replaced with an empty JSON
  object, while event identity, source, type, processing status, and errors stay.

## Records intentionally retained today

- The scrubbed user id/tombstone and Stripe customer id.
- Stripe subscription rows and billing/reconciliation metadata needed to
  reconcile or refund provider-side activity. Stripe itself retains invoices,
  payments, refunds, and customer records under Stripe/account policy.
- Aggregate or operational telemetry that is not removed by `PurgeUser`, such
  as usage/provider-call measurements, ingest-attempt diagnostics,
  reconciliation history/reports, logs, Sentry events, and PostHog events.
  Some of these may still contain a stable user, workspace, material, file,
  trace, job, or provider identifier even when direct profile fields are gone.
- Database backups, object-store versions/lifecycle remnants, logs, analytics,
  and provider copies can outlive the primary-row purge according to each
  external system's separate retention settings.

## Policy and implementation decisions still needed

- Define a retention period and legal/business purpose for each retained table,
  log stream, backup, analytics destination, and external provider.
- Decide which stable identifiers must be deleted, keyed-hashed, irreversibly
  aggregated, or rotated after account deletion. Plain replacement of profile
  fields is not sufficient anonymization when identifiers can be joined to
  billing, logs, support records, or provider data.
- Define which billing records must remain for tax, chargeback, refund, and
  accounting obligations, and separate those from product analytics data.
- Decide whether operational error payloads, webhook errors, reconciliation
  metadata, job payloads, and notification payloads require field-level
  redaction beyond the current purge.
- Decide how to handle Stripe webhook events that cannot yet be correlated to a
  user. Their raw payload cannot be scrubbed by user purge until correlation
  succeeds, so define a bounded retention/redaction rule for unmatched events.
- Define the external retention and deletion guarantees for synchronous
  ElevenLabs transcription requests; Capy Notebook stores no provider transcript
  id or webhook correlation row after the response completes.
- Inventory object storage, cache objects, collaboration persistence, vector/RAG
  tenants, parse/transcription providers, email delivery, Clerk, Stripe, Sentry,
  and PostHog. Record deletion APIs, retention guarantees, and retry/alert paths.
- Specify backup deletion/expiry and restoration handling so a backup restore
  cannot silently resurrect data whose deletion deadline has passed.
- Define an analytics schema that collects only approved aggregates at write
  time where possible, instead of retaining customer-level source data and
  attempting to anonymize it later.
- Add operator-visible audit/reporting for purge completion, external deletion
  failures, retention expiry, and anonymization jobs after the policy is agreed.
